Don't have an in-depth study handy, but as a general rule, you can't 
pray right after your god does something for you.  ie: after you've 
just prayed, or after you've been given an artifact after sacrificing.
The theory being (I suppose) that your god has just given you something
nice, and doesn't want to be pestered for a while.

The hand of Elbereth is seen as being a very good gift.  Once your god has
made you that, your prayer timeout increases a lot (ie: the interval between
prayers increases).

A good guideline is to never pray unless:
 a) you're desperate (ie: you'll die if you don't)
 b) you're *sure* your prayer timeout has expired.  The only way I know of
    to be sure of that is to sacrifice on a co-aligned alter, and see
    the sacrificed be accepted.  
The above apply particularly if you've been made the Hand of Elbereth.

So don't leave a co-aligned altar after getting a gift, until you've fixed
your prayer timeout, and don't pass any co-aligned alter without sacrificing 
at least one thing to check your timeout.  People who think recording the
time they pray is sufficient to determine when they can next pray will be
in trouble when their prayer timeout increases. 
